Lepton: faster copy-back (derived VA mapping on Mesa, threaded 10-bit conversion)

Profiling (LEPTON_VA_TIMING, patch 0028) on AMD RX 6700 XT showed the
download dominating: FFmpeg's av_hwframe_map(READ) creates a VA image for
every frame and copies the surface into it with vaGetImage. On Mesa that is
a new 12 to 25 MB allocation and a single-threaded CPU copy per 4K frame
(26 ms for 4K P010). FFmpeg avoids vaDeriveImage for reading because some
drivers (Intel's) map the surface uncached.

- Download: on Mesa's VA driver (vendor "Mesa Gallium driver"), map the
  surface itself (AV_HWFRAME_MAP_DIRECT): radeonsi reads it through a cached
  staging copy, 4 ms for 4K P010. Other drivers keep FFmpeg's map. If
  deriving fails, the session falls back to FFmpeg's map.
  debug.ffmpeg-codec2.copyback=auto|map|derive overrides (auto by default).
- GPU wait: vaSyncSurface before the download, timed separately (sync).
- Conversion: 10-bit frames of 1080p and larger convert on two swscale
  slice threads (P010 to 8-bit is swscale's generic path; three or more
  threads were no faster). 8-bit NV12 stays on one thread.
  debug.ffmpeg-codec2.convert-threads=0 (auto) or 1 to 8 overrides.
  Swscale's flags and output are unchanged (bit-identical luma).

The swscale call returns when every slice is done and the derived mapping
is released with the frame, so flush, seek, resolution changes and
release are unchanged.

